Subject: [PATCH 5/5] readline: leave a host's answer to a terminal query out of the keys

A program that asks the terminal something and ends before it reads the answer, or never reads it,
leaves the host's answer in the terminal's input, and Readline finds it at its prompt. Readline
decodes it as keys: an unbound sequence beeps and the rest of the answer is typed into the line. The
reader takes the answer out in two places, before the key lookup sees it. Where Readline reads a key
from the typeahead or the terminal, an Escape that begins an answer the host's query broker builds,
delivered whole, is taken with the rest of the answer and dropped; anything else is given back to
the typeahead untouched and in order. Where Readline asks whether input is waiting before it reads a
key, an answer at the front of the terminal's bytes is dropped first, so the check does not report
an answer as a key, and a check with a time limit goes on asking for what is left of the time it was
given. Nothing waits for a byte the terminal has not delivered, so no key a person presses is held
back. A program's own read (`read -e`), a quoted insertion and a bracketed paste are left alone. The
bytes of a bracketed paste's opening that the reader has read ahead are counted with the terminal's,
and read from the typeahead, so a paste in a minibuffer is still found whole.

